-- Q3861: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Tests of operating effectiveness determine whether controls operated as designed and consistently throughout the examination period, per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320). Unlike design tests that assess a point in time, operating effectiveness tests evaluate performance over a period and require evidence of consistent application.
Wrong (A): Assessing whether a control is properly designed at a point in time is a test of design effectiveness, not operating effectiveness, and applies to Type I rather than Type II engagements.
Wrong (C): Financial viability of the service organization is outside the scope of SOC 2 control testing, which focuses on trust services criteria.
Wrong (D): User entity satisfaction is not a testing objective in SOC engagements. Tests focus on whether controls meet specified criteria, not customer satisfaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3861;

-- Q3862: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Inspecting documentary evidence of completed access reviews, including sign-off records, review dates, and remediation actions, demonstrates that the control operated throughout the period per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320). Documentary evidence provides the strongest proof of consistent control execution across multiple quarters.
Wrong (A): Inquiry alone is insufficient for testing operating effectiveness. Management assertions must be corroborated with documentary or observational evidence.
Wrong (B): Observing a single review at period end provides evidence for only that one point in time and does not cover the full 12-month examination period.
Wrong (D): Reviewing only the policy document tests design effectiveness, not whether the control was actually performed throughout the period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3862;

-- Q3868: SOC Testing Controls [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320) and AICPA SOC guidance, when a new control is implemented during the Type II examination period, the auditor tests it only for the portion of the period it was in operation. The implementation date and any predecessor control must be clearly documented in the system description and report.
Wrong (B): The auditor cannot unilaterally extend the examination period. The period is defined in the engagement terms, and the new control is tested for its actual period of operation.
Wrong (C): Ignoring the new control would leave a gap in assurance. The auditor must evaluate all controls that were in operation during the examination period.
Wrong (D): Both the predecessor and successor controls should be tested for their respective periods of operation, not just the replaced control.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3868;

-- Q3870: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.39), observation provides evidence that a control is operating at the specific point in time the auditor witnesses it. A single observation on March 15 does not demonstrate consistent operation throughout the full 12-month examination period and must be supplemented with other procedures.
Wrong (A): Observation does provide evidence, even though the auditor''s presence may influence behavior. The limitation is temporal scope, not the validity of the evidence itself.
Wrong (C): A single observation cannot establish effectiveness for the entire period. Inspection of documentary evidence across multiple dates is needed for period-wide assurance.
Wrong (D): Observation provides evidence about the control''s operation, not merely the administrator''s qualifications. It shows the control was performed at the observed point in time.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3870;

-- Q3871: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.39), combining inquiry with inspection of documentary evidence provides the strongest evidence for operating effectiveness. Inquiry explains how the daily review is performed, while inspection of dated initials and backup logs for a sample spanning the full period confirms consistent execution.
Wrong (A): Inquiry alone provides only verbal assertions without corroboration. Management representations must be supplemented with documentary or observational evidence.
Wrong (B): Reading the policy document confirms the control is documented (design effectiveness) but does not demonstrate that the control was actually performed throughout the period.
Wrong (C): Observing one backup verification confirms the process for a single point in time but does not cover the full 12-month examination period.$EXPL$
